  "sourceFitAssessment": {
    "learnerTask": "Locate B2M in a class-I assembly and compare losing one allele with a shared assembly defect affecting multiple class-I targets.",
    "gap": "The support-piece analogy and text are strong, but no picture locates B2M or contrasts the breadth of these two defects. Reuse the existing APM/HLA physical reference with a small allele-versus-shared-dependency comparison rather than a separate elaborate image.",
    "accuracyGuardrails": [
      "A variant does not establish complete functional loss.",
      "Class II does not share this B2M dependency.",
      "Reduced class I is not sufficient proof of NK killing."
    ],
    "baselinePageSHA256": "c5d193b9402f0004f368d64ecd2399aeb7cf598525f73b82d127149b81a0f362",
    "currentVisualFit": "adequate",
    "humanReview": "pending",
    "reasoning": "The enlarged membrane complex explicitly puts peptide in the heavy-chain groove and B2M beside, rather than through, the membrane. Repeated illustrative alleles make one-allele loss visibly different from a shared assembly defect. Dashed impaired complexes are a conditional schematic, not a claimed frequency or diagnosis from a variant. The Class-II sentence and variant warning prevent extending the class-I dependency or equating sequence with functional loss. Reusing the processing diagram supplies the broader route; the extension supplies dependency breadth and local anatomy."
  },
